What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/powerdown
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Writing 1 powers the device down, writing 0 powers it up.
		Reading returns the current state. This is the inverse of the
		PWRON register bit, which is exposed this way so that the
		attribute matches the usual IIO powerdown convention.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_low_bias
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_mid_bias
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_high_bias
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/rf_amp1_bias
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/rf_amp2_bias
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/splitter1_bias
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/splitter2_bias
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/pa_bias
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Bias current trim codes for the three frequency multipliers,
		the two RF amplifier stages, the two splitter stages and the
		power amplifiers. Each is a raw 4-bit code, 0 to 15, with no
		documented transfer function to a current, so no unit is
		implied. Larger codes correspond to larger bias currents.

		The eight codes occupy four registers as nibble pairs, so the
		driver uses read-modify-write and writing one attribute does
		not disturb its neighbour.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/splitter1_en
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/splitter12_en
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/splitter34_en
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Enable (1) or disable (0) the first splitter stage, the
		splitter feeding outputs 1 and 2, and the splitter feeding
		outputs 3 and 4. These take effect only while the transmit
		sequencer is disabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/rf_amp_en
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Enable (1) or disable (0) the input RF amplifier. Takes effect
		only while the multiplier sequencer is disabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_low_state
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_mid_state
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_high_state
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Power state of the low, mid and high band frequency
		multipliers, one of the strings listed in the matching
		_available attribute:

		- "sleep": block powered down.
		- "ready": block biased but not driving the signal path.
		  Selecting a band from ready is faster than from sleep.
		- "active": block biased and driving the signal path.

		Exactly one band is normally active at a time. Reading
		returns -EIO if the hardware reports the undocumented fourth
		encoding of the underlying bit pair.

		These take effect only while the multiplier sequencer is
		disabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_low_state_available
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_mid_state_available
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_high_state_available
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Space separated list of the power states accepted by the
		matching attribute.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/bandpass_filter
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Selects which bandpass filter path the multiplier output takes,
		one of the strings listed in bandpass_filter_available: "low"
		or "high". Takes effect only while the multiplier sequencer is
		disabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/bandpass_filter_available
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Space separated list of the filter paths accepted by the
		matching attribute.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/pa_notch_en
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Enable (1) or disable (0) the notch filter in front of the
		power amplifiers. Takes effect only while the multiplier
		sequencer is disabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/attenuation
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Raw code for the 5-bit step attenuator, 0 to 31. The datasheet
		gives no code to decibel transfer function, so the value is
		deliberately unscaled and carries no unit. Larger codes
		correspond to more attenuation. Takes effect only while the
		multiplier sequencer is disabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/adc_clock_frequency
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Clock frequency in Hz used by the on-chip 8-bit ADC, one of
		the two values listed in adc_clock_frequency_available.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/adc_clock_frequency_available
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Space separated list of the ADC clock frequencies in Hz
		accepted by adc_clock_frequency.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/tx_sequencer_en
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_sequencer_en
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Enable (1) or disable (0) the transmit or multiplier sequencer.

		While a sequencer is enabled the path it owns is driven from
		its mode table and the matching manual attributes have no
		effect. The driver couples the control latch to this bit:
		enabling a sequencer puts the latch in circuit, disabling it
		bypasses the latch so that manual writes reach the blocks
		without an external sequencer pin pulse.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/tx_sequencer_depth
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Number of transmit sequencer states in the loop, 1 to 70. The
		sequencer steps state 1, 2, ... up to this value and then
		wraps back to state 1. State 0 is outside the loop and is
		entered only by tx_sequencer_reset.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_sequencer_depth
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Number of multiplier sequencer states in the loop, 1 to 16.
		The sequencer steps state 1, 2, ... up to this value and then
		wraps back to state 1. State 0 is outside the loop and is
		entered only by multiplier_sequencer_reset.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/tx_sleep_control
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_sleep_control
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Writing 1 routes the external sleep pin to the transmit or the
		multiplier sequencer. At most one of the two may be set; an
		attempt to set one while the other is already set is rejected
		with -EINVAL rather than silently clearing the other.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/tx_sleep_hold
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_sleep_hold
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Writing 1 makes the sequencer hold its current state while the
		sleep input is asserted, instead of resetting to state 0.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/tx_sequencer_advance
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_sequencer_advance
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Write-only. Writing 1 steps the sequencer on by one state.
		Writing 0 is rejected with -EINVAL.

		If the devicetree describes the external advance pin, the
		driver pulses that pin. Otherwise it drives the SPI bit high
		and then low, because the datasheet does not state that the
		bit self-clears.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/tx_sequencer_reset
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_sequencer_reset
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Write-only. Writing 1 returns the sequencer to state 0, which
		always applies mode 0. Writing 0 is rejected with -EINVAL.

		If the devicetree describes the external reset pin, the driver
		pulses that pin. Otherwise it drives the SPI bit high and then
		low, because the datasheet does not state that the bit
		self-clears.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/tx_current_state
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_current_state
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Read-only. State the sequencer is currently pointing at.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/tx_current_mode
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_current_mode
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Read-only. Mode, 0 to 15, that the current state selects.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/tx_sequencer_table
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Binary attribute, 70 bytes, holding the transmit sequencer
		state table. One byte per state, each byte a mode index 0 to
		15. The file offset is the state number minus one: offset 0 is
		state 1 and offset 69 is state 70. State 0 is hardwired to
		mode 0 in the hardware, has no register field, and so is not
		represented in the file.

		Reads return the whole table. Writes may start at any offset
		and cover any length; only the states actually covered are
		reprogrammed. The hardware packs two states per register and
		the driver does the packing, using read-modify-write where a
		write covers only one of a register's two states.

		If any byte of the write buffer exceeds 15 the whole write
		fails with -EINVAL before any register is touched.

		Writing the table does not change tx_sequencer_depth.

		Load a whole table::

			cat table.bin > tx_sequencer_table

		Change state 5 only::

			printf '\x03' | dd of=tx_sequencer_table bs=1 \
				seek=4 conv=notrunc

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_sequencer_table
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Binary attribute, 16 bytes, holding the multiplier sequencer
		state table. Same format, offset convention and write
		semantics as tx_sequencer_table: offset 0 is state 1 and
		offset 15 is state 16.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/tx_mode_table
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Binary attribute, 32 bytes, holding the transmit mode table.
		Two bytes per mode, so mode m occupies offsets 2 * m and
		2 * m + 1. The sequencer applies the row when it enters a state
		selecting that mode.

		Even byte, the power amplifier states, one two bit field per
		output. Bits 7:6 are output 0, 5:4 output 1, 3:2 output 2 and
		1:0 output 3. Each field is 0x0 for sleep, 0x2 for ready and
		0x3 for active, matching out_altvoltageY_pa_state. 0x1 is
		undocumented and rejected.

		Odd byte, the splitter enables. Bit 2 is splitter1_en, bit 1 is
		splitter12_en and bit 0 is splitter34_en. Bits 7:3 are unused.

		Reads return the whole table. Writes may start at any offset and
		cover any length; only the bytes covered are reprogrammed.
		Writing both bytes of a row in one call makes the mode update
		atomic against the sequencer.

		If any even byte of the write buffer contains a 0x1 field the
		whole write fails with -EINVAL before any register is touched.

		Program mode 3 as output 0 active, the rest asleep, with
		splitter1 and splitter12 enabled::

			printf '\xc0\x06' | dd of=tx_mode_table bs=1 seek=6 \
				conv=notrunc

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/multiplier_mode_table
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Binary attribute, 32 bytes, holding the multiplier mode table.
		Same two bytes per mode layout, offset convention and write
		semantics as tx_mode_table.

		Even byte, bit 6 is rf_amp_en, bits 5:4 are the low multiplier
		state, 3:2 the mid state and 1:0 the high state. The two bit
		state fields use the same encoding as in tx_mode_table and match
		multiplier_low_state, multiplier_mid_state and
		multiplier_high_state. Bit 7 is unused.

		Odd byte, bit 7 is bandpass_filter, 0 for "high" and 1 for
		"low". Bit 6 is pa_notch_en. Bits 4:0 are attenuation. Bit 5 is
		unused.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/out_altvoltageY_pa_state
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Power state of the power amplifier on output Y, one of the
		strings listed in out_altvoltageY_pa_state_available: "sleep",
		"ready" or "active". The three states have the same meaning as
		for multiplier_low_state. Takes effect only while the transmit
		sequencer is disabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_powerY_en
KernelVersion:
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Enable (1) or disable (0) the RF power detector on output Y.
		The detectors are off after reset and draw current when on,
		so the driver leaves them under explicit userspace control
		rather than enabling one behind a read of in_powerY_raw.
		in_powerY_raw is only meaningful once this is set.

		in_powerY_raw is the raw 8-bit detector code from the shared
		on-chip ADC. No in_powerY_scale or in_powerY_offset is
		provided, because the datasheet gives no transfer function
		from the detector code to an RF power and the response is
		frequency dependent, so the reading is a relative indication
		only.
